ML_PAGE("l7-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l7\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 7 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 7 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l7\" data-quiz=\"7\">Start Chapter 7 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l7\" id=\"l7p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Practice questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s4\" title=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\">Formula map and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l7\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 7 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 7 - Analyzing Common Stocks: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 50 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are study suggestions, not confirmed examination requirements. Choose the single best answer; check the separate key only after attempting the questions. Original scenarios use the stated conventions; accounting and ratio definitions may vary between courses and data sources.</p>\n<p>The reasoning sequence is <strong>economic setting → industry conditions → company evidence → interpretation → investment judgment</strong>. A ratio is evidence about a question, not a complete investment recommendation.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l7p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>Which sequence best represents the chapter's traditional top-down security analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Company → industry → economy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Economy → industry → company</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Portfolio beta → shareholder vote → dividend date</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market price → share count → broker selection</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Top-down analysis moves from the economy to industry conditions to a particular company.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beginning with a company's accounting figures is not the traditional top-down sequence.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>An analyst estimates a stock's intrinsic value at RM14 when its market price is RM11. What does this comparison establish?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The analyst estimates the stock is undervalued, subject to assumptions and risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its future price is guaranteed to reach RM14.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The company's accounting book value must equal RM14.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every investor should buy, regardless of circumstances.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The estimate exceeds the current price by RM3, but depends on forecasts, required return and risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intrinsic value is an estimate, not a guaranteed future price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A firm raises sales after an economic expansion that investors widely anticipated months earlier. Why might its stock still fall when the result is announced?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The result may be weaker than the higher growth investors had already priced in.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Stock prices never reflect expectations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rising sales always require falling earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A positive economic result legally forces stock prices down.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Prices can respond to the difference between the result and prior expectations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Positive growth does not necessarily mean a positive surprise.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Which pair correctly identifies examples of fiscal and monetary policy, respectively?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Government changes expenditure; government changes tax rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Central bank changes its policy rate; a company pays a dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Government changes expenditure; central bank changes its policy interest rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An individual buys shares; a company raises prices.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Government spending is fiscal policy; central-bank interest-rate policy is monetary policy.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Tax and government expenditure are both fiscal examples.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A central bank raises interest rates. Which company deserves particular analysis of this change?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A firm cannot be affected unless it is a bank.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every company will necessarily experience the same change in earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A highly indebted property developer whose demand and financing costs are rate-sensitive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Any company with shares, because rates determine its exact stock price mechanically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Higher rates can raise refinancing costs and affect rate-sensitive property demand; the magnitude depends on contracts and conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An economic driver can affect firms differently.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Which economic measure most directly tracks the market value of final goods and services produced within an economy over a period?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Book value per share</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Gross domestic product</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dividend payout ratio</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Current ratio</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>GDP measures the market value of final goods and services produced domestically during a period.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> GDP is a flow, not company equity or a stock-price ratio.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>An industry is young, products are unproven, and future market adoption is highly uncertain. Which lifecycle stage best fits the chapter's framework?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Initial development</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mature growth</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Stability by definition</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Guaranteed decline</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Initial development is characterized by a new industry, uncertain adoption and substantial risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A young industry is not guaranteed to mature successfully.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Industry sales are growing rapidly, but many new firms are entering and prices are falling. What is the best conclusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Industry growth ensures every firm's profit margin will rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Company analysis is unnecessary in a growing industry.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Competition and pricing power must be assessed alongside industry growth.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Rapid growth guarantees every stock is cheap.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Sales expansion can coexist with intense entry, lower prices and weaker margins.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A good industry growth rate does not ensure good company profitability or a low stock price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A December 31 balance sheet primarily describes:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Cash sales throughout December only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only next year's predicted dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Revenue and expenses over an entire financial year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and shareholders' equity as of a date.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse a snapshot with an annual flow statement.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Which statement best distinguishes the income statement from the cash-flow statement?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both always show the same net amount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The income statement measures profit for a period; the cash-flow statement tracks cash inflows and outflows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An income statement is a point-in-time snapshot of assets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Borrowing money is revenue in every accounting context.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Accrual profit and actual cash movements answer related but different questions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Profit and cash need not coincide in amount or timing.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A firm records RM100,000 in credit sales at year-end. The customers have not yet paid. Which is most accurate, assuming recognition criteria are met?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Revenue and receivables may increase even though cash has not yet been collected.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Cash must increase by RM100,000 when revenue is recorded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Liabilities must increase by RM100,000 because sales are recorded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No revenue can ever be recognized before cash is received.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Recognized credit sales can increase revenue and accounts receivable while cash is unchanged until collection.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Recognized revenue does not always mean cash has arrived.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A company reports rising net income while cash from operations declines and receivables grow much faster than sales. Which action is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ignore receivables because reported profits eliminate liquidity risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Conclude immediately that fraud is proven.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Investigate credit terms, collection quality and the reasons cash and profit differ.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Conclude that every receivable is uncollectible.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>These trends justify investigating working capital, cash conversion and any accounting explanations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A warning signal is not proof of fraud or of uncollectibility.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Current assets total RM600,000 and current liabilities total RM300,000. What is the current ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 200,000 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM300,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.0 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Current ratio = RM600,000 / RM300,000 = <strong>2.0 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A ratio is unitless, whereas working capital is a monetary amount.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Using the Q13 figures, net working capital is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM300,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM900,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM180 million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Net working capital = RM600,000 - RM300,000 = <strong>RM300,000</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtract; do not reuse the current ratio as a monetary figure.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A firm's current ratio rises from 1.5 to 2.5, mainly because slow-moving inventory has accumulated. What is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Liquidity has certainly improved in every respect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The firm has no short-term obligations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The headline ratio rose, but inventory quality and cash conversion require investigation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The rise guarantees a higher share price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The ratio includes inventory, but slow-moving stock may not convert to cash promptly or at its stated value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher headline ratio does not establish the quality of current assets.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Annual credit sales are RM1.2 million and average accounts receivable are RM200,000. Using credit sales / average receivables, what is receivables turnover?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.17 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 12 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Receivables turnover = RM1.2m / RM0.2m = <strong>6 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the stated credit-sales and average-balance convention; check the denominator.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Annual cost of goods sold is RM900,000 and average inventory is RM150,000. Using cost of goods sold / average inventory, inventory turnover is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.17 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM750,000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Inventory turnover = RM900,000 / RM150,000 = <strong>6 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The numerator here is cost of goods sold, not sales revenue.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A firm's inventory turnover jumps after it reduces inventory substantially. Which further question is most useful before calling this an unqualified improvement?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Does any inventory appear on any other firm's balance sheet?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Is its par value identical to its share price?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Did it change its company name?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Did shortages cause missed sales or weaken service?</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Less inventory can improve turnover but can also cause shortages or lost sales.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher turnover is not universally beneficial.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Annual sales are RM3 million and average total assets are RM1.5 million. Total asset turnover is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 4.5 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Asset turnover = RM3m / RM1.5m = <strong>2.0 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Sales is the numerator; assets are the denominator.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Using total liabilities as the question's debt measure, a company has liabilities of RM800,000 and equity of RM400,000. Its debt-to-equity ratio is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3.0 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Debt-to-equity = RM800,000 / RM400,000 = <strong>2.0 times</strong> using the defined debt measure.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Some sources use interest-bearing debt instead of total liabilities; follow the definition supplied.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>A company has annual EBIT of RM500,000 and interest expense of RM100,000. Its times-interest-earned ratio is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.2 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 6 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Times interest earned = EBIT / interest expense = RM500,000 / RM100,000 = <strong>5 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> EBIT, not revenue or equity, is the specified coverage numerator.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Sales are RM2 million and net profit is RM160,000 for the year. What is net profit margin?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 80%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Net margin = RM160,000 / RM2,000,000 = <strong>8%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide profit by sales, then express the result as a percentage.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A company earns RM120,000 net income over a year. Average total assets are RM1 million and average equity is RM600,000. Using net income divided by each average balance, what are ROA and ROE?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ROA 12%; ROE 12%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ROA 20%; ROE 12%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ROA 8%; ROE 40%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ROA 12%; ROE 20%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>ROA = RM120,000 / RM1,000,000 = <strong>12%</strong>; ROE = RM120,000 / RM600,000 = <strong>20%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish the assets and equity denominators and use the stated average balances.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>A firm's annual net profit margin is 5%, sales are RM2 million, and average assets are RM1 million. Using net income / average assets, what is ROA?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 2.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 40%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Asset turnover = RM2m / RM1m = 2; ROA = margin x turnover = $5\\%\\times2=10\\%$. Equivalently, net income is RM100,000 and RM100,000 / RM1m = 10%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Profit margin alone is not ROA.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Two otherwise comparable companies have the same ROA, but Company B finances a larger portion of its assets with debt. What is the best inference?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B's higher debt guarantees higher profits in all conditions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> B may have higher ROE through leverage, but it may also face more financial risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both must have identical ROE regardless of their equity balances.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Debt cannot influence returns to shareholders.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With positive equity and the stated comparable conditions, leverage can raise the equity multiplier and ROE while adding obligations and downside risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher ROE alone does not prove stronger operations or a safer firm.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A stock trades at RM30 and has trailing annual earnings per share of RM2. Its trailing P/E is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 60 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 28 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 15 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Trailing P/E = RM30 / RM2 = <strong>15 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use earnings per share for the same specified basis; trailing and forward estimates differ.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Company A has a lower P/E than Company B. Which conclusion is best supported by that fact alone?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A costs less per unit of its stated earnings, but growth, risk, accounting, and earnings quality require further analysis.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A is certainly undervalued and will outperform B.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A must have the lower share price in currency units.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A's earnings are guaranteed to rise.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A lower P/E is a lower price per unit of the specified earnings, not proof of mispricing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Growth, risk and sustainability can justify different multiples.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A company has a P/E of 18 and expected annual earnings growth of 12%. If the course convention expresses growth as the whole-number percentage 12, its PEG is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.67</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 150</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Using the question's convention, PEG = 18 / 12 = <strong>1.5</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the stated percentage convention; PEG is a rough comparison, not proof of fair value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A company has common equity of RM8 million, 2 million common shares, and a share price of RM10. What is price-to-book?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.4 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4.0 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.5 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Book value per share = RM8m / 2m = RM4; P/B = RM10 / RM4 = <strong>2.5 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide market price per share by book value per share; a low P/B is not automatically cheap.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Company X shows growing sales, declining operating cash flow, rising receivables and a rising debt-to-equity ratio. Which investment conclusion is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sales growth alone makes X an attractive investment at any price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The debt-to-equity ratio establishes intrinsic value without other inputs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Any increase in receivables proves the company is insolvent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Further investigate cash collection, leverage, financing capacity and valuation before deciding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The combination raises questions about collections, cash needs and debt service; further analysis and valuation are needed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A single positive or negative measure does not settle a complex investment decision.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l7p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula map and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l7_ratio_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Choose the ratio family from the question you are asking, not the other way round.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Current ratio</strong> = current assets / current liabilities; <strong>net working capital</strong> = current assets - current liabilities.</li>\n<li>Under the conventions used here: <strong>receivables turnover</strong> = annual credit sales / average receivables; <strong>inventory turnover</strong> = annual cost of goods sold / average inventory; <strong>asset turnover</strong> = annual sales / average assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Debt-to-equity</strong> = stated debt measure / equity; <strong>interest coverage</strong> = EBIT / interest expense. Always check whether the data provider defines debt as total liabilities or interest-bearing debt.</li>\n<li><strong>Net margin</strong> = net income / sales; <strong>ROA</strong> = net income / average assets; <strong>ROE</strong> = net income / average equity; <strong>ROA</strong> = margin x asset turnover when periods and denominators are consistent.</li>\n<li><strong>P/E</strong> = price per share / EPS; <strong>P/B</strong> = price per share / book value per common share. <strong>PEG</strong> here uses P/E divided by the whole-number annual growth percentage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Classify mistakes as statement/period confusion, wrong numerator or denominator, arithmetic, unsupported inference, or failure to compare trends and peers. Then ask what evidence would change the conclusion; do not equate a ratio with a complete valuation.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Questions and numbers are original and use the course concepts in Chapter 7, <em>Analyzing Common Stocks</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). Ratio conventions are stated because financial-data providers and the course may use different definitions or balances. Practice targets are not official exam requirements.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l7-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 7 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>On the course slides, which pair of ratios uses annual <strong>sales</strong> as the numerator?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The current ratio and the net working capital figure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Receivables turnover and inventory turnover</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Return on assets and return on equity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The P/E ratio and the price-to-book ratio</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Slides 7-34 and 7-35 define receivables turnover as annual sales over accounts receivable and inventory turnover as annual sales over inventory (total asset turnover also uses sales). The common alternatives, credit sales over average receivables and cost of goods sold over average inventory, must be stated if used. ROA and ROE use net profit after taxes, and P/E and P/B use the share price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which development is on slide 7-54's list of signs that a company could be heading for financial problems?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A payout ratio that keeps rising while EPS stays stable for several years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A P/E ratio below the industry average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A rising total asset turnover</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Cash flow from operations dropping below net income</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The slide lists receivables and inventories growing faster than sales, a falling current ratio, a high and rising debt-equity ratio, operating cash flow below net income, and indecipherable off-balance-sheet items. A low P/E (B) is a valuation signal, not a listed warning sign, and a rising asset turnover (C) usually means better asset efficiency.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In which order does the chapter's industry growth cycle run?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Initial development, rapid expansion, mature growth, stability or decline</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rapid expansion, initial development, stability, mature growth</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Mature growth, rapid expansion, initial development, decline</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Initial development, mature growth, rapid expansion, then stability or decline</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Slide 7-19: a new, high-risk industry (initial development) gains acceptance (rapid expansion), then grows roughly with the economy with more predictable returns (mature growth), and finally faces flat or shrinking demand (stability or decline). It is a pattern, not a timetable every industry must follow.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Apex's current ratio fell from $2.00$ to $1.56$ while its net working capital rose from $\\text{RM}40$ million to $\\text{RM}45$ million. How can both be true?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Working capital is measured in \"times\", so it cannot be compared with the current ratio at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Working capital excludes inventory, while the current ratio includes it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Current liabilities grew proportionally faster than current assets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It cannot be true; one of the figures must contain an error.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Current assets went from $80$ to $125$ ($+56\\%$) and current liabilities from $40$ to $80$ ($+100\\%$). The difference grew ($125 - 80 = 45$), but the ratio fell ($\\dfrac{125}{80} = 1.56$). A ratio and a difference answer different questions. Working capital is in ringgit (A is backwards), and both measures include inventory (B).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the chapter warn that a high ROE does not, on its own, prove that operations have improved?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A bigger equity multiplier can lift ROE while margin and turnover stay flat.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because ROE is calculated before tax, so it overstates the profit that is actually available to shareholders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because ROE uses the market value of equity, which moves with the share price rather than with operations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because ROE always equals ROA, so it adds no information about operations.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The DuPont identity separates operations (margin x turnover = ROA) from financing (the equity multiplier). In the notes, two firms with the same $10\\%$ ROA show ROEs of $12.5\\%$ and $25\\%$ purely because of leverage. The course ROE uses net profit after taxes (B) and book equity (C), and ROE equals ROA only for an all-equity firm (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Fuel prices jump. An airline's shares fall while an oil producer's rise. Which principle of the top-down approach does this illustrate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Economic changes affect all stocks roughly equally, so the gap between the two shares must be random noise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fuel prices are a company-specific matter, so economic analysis should leave them to the company analyst.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Once the economy has been analysed well, industry and company analysis add little to the forecast.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The same change hits industries through different mechanisms, so link it to the industry and firm.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The notes stress that a macroeconomic observation becomes useful only once it is tied to a mechanism. Even two airlines can be affected differently through fuel hedging, routes and debt. A, B and C each drop a level of the economy → industry → company chain.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Current assets are $\\text{RM}5.6$ million, of which inventory is $\\text{RM}2.1$ million; current liabilities are $\\text{RM}3.5$ million. What are the current ratio and net working capital?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $1.0$ times and $\\text{RM}0$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $1.6$ times and $\\text{RM}2.1$ million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $0.625$ times and $\\text{RM}2.1$ million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $1.6$ times and $\\text{RM}3.5$ million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Current ratio $= \\dfrac{5.6}{3.5} = 1.6$ times; net working capital $= 5.6 - 3.5 = \\text{RM}2.1$ million. A is the quick ratio, $\\dfrac{5.6 - 2.1}{3.5} = 1.0$, which removes inventory; C inverts the ratio ($\\dfrac{3.5}{5.6}$); D repeats current liabilities instead of subtracting them. That working capital happens to equal inventory here is a coincidence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>All of a firm's $\\text{RM}14.6$ million of annual sales are on credit. Receivables were $\\text{RM}1.6$ million at the start of the year and $\\text{RM}2.4$ million at the end. Using credit sales over <strong>average</strong> receivables and a $365$-day year, what are receivables turnover and the average collection period?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $6.08$ times and $60$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $9.13$ times and $40$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $7.3$ times and $50$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $0.14$ times and $2{,}664$ days</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Average receivables $= \\dfrac{1.6 + 2.4}{2} = 2.0$; turnover $= \\dfrac{14.6}{2.0} = 7.3$ times; collection period $\\approx \\dfrac{365}{7.3} = 50$ days. A uses the ending balance (the slide convention, not the one asked for), B the beginning balance, and D inverts the ratio.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Net income is $\\text{RM}42$ million, preferred dividends are $\\text{RM}6$ million, there are $15$ million weighted-average common shares, and the share price is $\\text{RM}31.20$. What are EPS and the P/E ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}2.40$ and $13.0$ times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}2.80$ and $11.1$ times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}2.40$ and $0.077$ times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}3.20$ and $9.75$ times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$EPS = \\dfrac{42 - 6}{15} = \\text{RM}2.40$ and $P/E = \\dfrac{31.20}{2.40} = 13$ times. B forgets to deduct preferred dividends ($\\dfrac{42}{15} = 2.80$), C divides EPS by price (the earnings yield), and D adds the preferred dividends instead of subtracting them.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Year 1: net margin $8\\%$, total asset turnover $1.5$, equity multiplier $2.0$. Year 2: net margin $6\\%$, total asset turnover $1.6$, equity multiplier $3.0$. What best describes the change in ROE?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ROE rose to $28.8\\%$ because the improvement in profit margins carried through to shareholders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ROE fell from $24\\%$ to $9.6\\%$, a sign of operating deterioration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ROE rose mainly because the gain in asset turnover outweighed the lower margin and the extra leverage.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ROA fell to $9.6\\%$; ROE rose to $28.8\\%$ only because leverage rose.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Year 1: $ROA = 8\\% \\times 1.5 = 12\\%$ and $ROE = 12\\% \\times 2 = 24\\%$. Year 2: $ROA = 6\\% \\times 1.6 = 9.6\\%$ and $ROE = 9.6\\% \\times 3 = 28.8\\%$. The margin fell (A is wrong), $9.6\\%$ is the new ROA, not ROE (B), and the small rise in turnover could not offset the lower margin (C).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Sales rose from $\\text{RM}50$ million to $\\text{RM}55$ million and net income from $\\text{RM}3.0$ million to $\\text{RM}3.3$ million. Receivables went from $\\text{RM}5$ million to $\\text{RM}8$ million; inventory from $\\text{RM}6$ million to $\\text{RM}6.6$ million. Operating cash flow fell from $\\text{RM}4$ million to $\\text{RM}1.5$ million. Which inference is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Inventory is the main problem, because stock has grown faster than sales and is absorbing the cash.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Slower collection: slide receivables turnover fell from $10$ to about $6.9$; investigate credit terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fraud is proven, because operating cash flow fell while reported profit rose over the same year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The net profit margin collapsed this year, which is what explains the fall in operating cash flow.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Receivables grew $60\\%$ against sales growth of $10\\%$ ($\\dfrac{50}{5} = 10$ to $\\dfrac{55}{8} = 6.875$ times), while inventory grew exactly in line with sales ($10\\%$), so A is wrong. The margin stayed at $\\dfrac{3.0}{50} = \\dfrac{3.3}{55} = 6\\%$ (D). A warning sign is not proof of fraud (C).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A firm's times interest earned is $6$ times, but its operating cash flow is negative and a $\\text{RM}40$ million bond matures next year. What is the best analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Coverage of $6$ times proves that the firm can meet all of its debt obligations, including the maturing bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Times interest earned already includes scheduled principal repayments, so the maturing bond is covered too.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is an earnings cover for interest only; principal and cash lie outside it, so refinancing risk remains.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Negative operating cash flow means times interest earned must also be negative.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{interest}}$ is an earnings cushion for interest only. EBIT is not cash, and principal repayments are not in the ratio (the notes say so explicitly). EBIT can be positive while operating cash flow is negative (D), for example when working capital absorbs cash.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An analyst writes: \"The stock's P/E is $8$ against an industry average of $16$, so it is undervalued by half. Buy.\" How should this be evaluated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsupported: a low P/E may reflect weak growth, high risk or inflated earnings; value must be estimated.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct, because P/E ratios always revert towards the industry average, so the share price must roughly double.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, provided the company's EPS is positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wrong, because a higher P/E always means a cheaper stock relative to its earnings and growth.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The notes: \"a low P/E can reflect undervaluation, weak prospects or temporary earnings\". The P/E compares price with one period's earnings; it does not establish intrinsic value. Nothing guarantees reversion (B), positive EPS only makes the ratio meaningful (C), and a higher P/E means a higher price per ringgit of earnings (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>After cutting stock levels, a retailer's inventory turnover rose from $5$ to $9$ times. Over the same year sales fell $12\\%$ and back-orders doubled. Management calls the higher turnover \"an unambiguous improvement\". Evaluate the claim.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct, because the slides state that a higher inventory turnover ratio is better and a lower one is worse.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Irrelevant: inventory turnover only measures how fast customers pay.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Overstated: turnover rose partly because stock was too thin, at the cost of lost sales.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Impossible: turnover cannot rise when sales fall.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notes warn that very fast turnover can mean insufficient stock. Here the lower denominator outweighed the lower sales, so turnover rose (D is wrong), but service levels and sales suffered. \"Higher is better\" on the slide is a rule of thumb, not a verdict (A). B describes receivables turnover.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A data screen shows Firm P's debt-equity ratio as $0.4$ (calculated from long-term debt) and Firm Q's as $1.1$ (calculated from total liabilities). A student concludes that \"Q is nearly three times as leveraged as P\". Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Valid, because both ratios are divided by stockholders' equity and are therefore directly comparable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Not valid: the numerators differ, so recalculate both on one debt definition first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Valid, as long as both firms have positive equity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Not valid, because a debt-equity ratio can never exceed $1$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The notes: \"identify the numerator before comparing\". Total liabilities include payables and other obligations that long-term debt excludes, so Q's ratio is mechanically larger. Positive equity (C) is necessary but not sufficient, and debt-equity can exceed $1$ (D), as in the practice paper's $\\dfrac{160}{80} = 2.0$.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor bought a stock on the thesis \"ROE is rising because operations are improving\". Which monitoring rule tests that thesis best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Track the share price weekly and sell after any fall of $10\\%$, since the market price already summarises everything about the firm's operations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Track the reported ROE every year and stay invested while it keeps rising.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Track the dividend per share and treat any dividend increase as confirmation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split ROE each year into margin, turnover and multiplier; flag rises driven only by the multiplier, and check cash conversion.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Only the DuPont split separates operating improvement from leverage, and the cash-conversion check guards against accrual profit. B is exactly the trap the thesis must avoid, A and C watch outcomes that need not reflect operations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You want an example in which the current ratio <strong>improves</strong> while true liquidity <strong>worsens</strong>. Which set of figures ($\\text{RM}$ million) shows it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Current assets rise from $3.0$ (cash $1.0$, receivables $1.0$, inventory $1.0$) to $4.5$ (cash $0.4$, receivables $1.0$, inventory $3.1$ of slow-moving stock); current liabilities rise from $2.0$ to $2.5$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Current assets and current liabilities both double, from $3.0$ and $2.0$ to $6.0$ and $4.0$, as the firm grows quickly and buys more stock on credit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Cash rises from $1.0$ to $2.0$ while current liabilities fall from $2.0$ to $1.5$; receivables, inventory and everything else on the balance sheet stay unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Current liabilities rise from $2.0$ to $3.0$ while current assets rise only from $3.0$ to $3.3$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>In A the current ratio rises from $\\dfrac{3.0}{2.0} = 1.5$ to $\\dfrac{4.5}{2.5} = 1.8$, but cash falls and the quick ratio falls from $\\dfrac{3.0 - 1.0}{2.0} = 1.0$ to $\\dfrac{4.5 - 3.1}{2.5} = 0.56$: the \"improvement\" is unsold stock. B leaves the ratio unchanged at $1.5$, C is a genuine improvement, and D makes the ratio fall.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Apex trades at $\\text{RM}12$; an analyst's estimate of value is $\\text{RM}15$. Its receivables and inventory are growing far faster than sales, operating cash flow is well below net income, and management plans extra borrowing. Which recommendation is best constructed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy now: the $25\\%$ upside to the analyst's estimate is large enough to more than compensate for any working-capital or financing issues.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Defer a firm buy: check receivables ageing, stock and loan terms; buy if collections normalise and a cautious value still exceeds $\\text{RM}12$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sell immediately: receivables growth of $66.67\\%$ against sales growth of $5\\%$ proves that the firm is already in financial distress.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy, because Apex's ROE of $30\\%$ shows it is a high-quality business whatever the price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A strong answer is conditional and names the evidence that would change it, as in the model answer to Question 6. A treats an estimate as a certainty, C treats a warning sign as proof, and D ignores both leverage (Apex's equity multiplier is $2.75$) and price.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A credit analyst works quarterly. Credit sales for a $90$-day quarter were $\\text{RM}9.0$ million and ending receivables are $\\text{RM}4.5$ million. What is days sales outstanding (DSO) for the quarter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $20$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $182.5$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $45$ days</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $2$ days</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$DSO = \\dfrac{\\text{receivables}}{\\text{credit sales}} \\times \\text{days in the period} = \\dfrac{4.5}{9.0} \\times 90 = 45$ days, equivalently $\\dfrac{90}{\\text{quarterly turnover of } 2}$. B mixes a quarter's sales with a $365$-day year; D is the turnover itself, not days.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>The five-step DuPont split is $ROE = \\dfrac{NI}{EBT} \\times \\dfrac{EBT}{EBIT} \\times \\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{sales}} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{sales}}{\\text{assets}} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{assets}}{\\text{equity}}$. A firm has tax burden $0.75$, interest burden $0.80$, EBIT margin $12\\%$, asset turnover $1.25$ and equity multiplier $2.0$. What is its ROE?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $18.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $9.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $7.2\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $24.0\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$0.75 \\times 0.80 \\times 0.12 \\times 1.25 \\times 2.0 = 0.18$. The first three terms multiply to the net profit margin of the three-step version ($7.2\\%$, option C), so the three-step and five-step versions agree. B is ROA (no equity multiplier) and D leaves out the tax burden.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A distributor collects receivables in $50$ days, holds inventory for $70$ days and pays suppliers in $40$ days. Its main supplier cuts the credit period to $25$ days. What happens to the cash conversion cycle (receivable days + inventory days - payable days)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It falls to $65$ days, because the distributor now pays its suppliers sooner.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It stays at $80$ days, because sales are unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It rises to $160$ days.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It rises from $80$ to $95$ days, tying up more working capital.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Before: $50 + 70 - 40 = 80$ days; after: $50 + 70 - 25 = 95$ days. Supplier credit funds part of the operating cycle; losing $15$ days of it means cash is tied up longer, which the firm must fund from cash, borrowing or slower growth. This is the payables line in the notes' profit-versus-cash reconciliation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A company earns net income of $30$. Its equity was $100$ at the start of the year and $200$ at the end, because it issued new shares on the last day of the year. Which ROE best describes the year's performance?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ROE on ending equity, $15\\%$, because the course slides always divide by year-end balances.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ROE on average equity, $20\\%$, which better matches the capital used during the year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both conventions give the same answer, $15\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ROE on beginning equity, $30\\%$, because that is always the correct convention for any firm.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\dfrac{30}{(100 + 200)/2} = 20\\%$ against $\\dfrac{30}{200} = 15\\%$. Profit was earned on roughly $100$ of equity for most of the year, so the ending balance badly mismatches flow and stock. The notes say averages often approximate the resources used better, but a question's stated convention still decides the exam answer.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>Three firms in a cyclical industry: L has ROA $7.5\\%$ and equity multiplier $4$; M has ROA $16\\%$ and multiplier $1.5$; N has ROA $10\\%$ and multiplier $2.5$. A downturn cuts every firm's ROA by $5$ percentage points, and multipliers stay the same. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> M suffers most, because its high ROA has the furthest to fall: its ROE drops to $16.5\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> N suffers most, because it sits in the middle on both drivers: its ROE drops to $12.5\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> L suffers most: its ROE falls by $20$ points, from $30\\%$ to $10\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All three ROEs fall by the same $5$ points.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$ROE = ROA \\times EM$. L: $30\\% \\to 2.5\\% \\times 4 = 10\\%$ ($-20$ points). M: $24\\% \\to 11\\% \\times 1.5 = 16.5\\%$ ($-7.5$). N: $25\\% \\to 5\\% \\times 2.5 = 12.5\\%$ ($-12.5$). The firm with the highest ROE before the downturn was also the most fragile: leverage magnifies falls as well as rises.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A common earnings-quality measure is the accrual ratio, $\\dfrac{NI - CFO}{\\text{average total assets}}$. For the chapter's integrated case (net income $1.2$, operating cash flow $0.4$, total assets $5.5$ and $8.0$, in $\\text{RM}$ million), what is it, and what does it suggest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About $11.9\\%$: much of the profit is accruals, mostly working-capital build-up.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $10.0\\%$, using ending total assets, which indicates unusually high earnings quality for the year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About $-11.9\\%$, meaning cash flow exceeded earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $66.7\\%$, using net income as the denominator, which proves that the earnings were manipulated.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\dfrac{1.2 - 0.4}{(5.5 + 8.0)/2} = \\dfrac{0.8}{6.75} \\approx 11.9\\%$. High accruals are a flag to investigate, consistent with slide 7-54's \"operating cash below net income\", not proof of manipulation (D, which also divides by net income). The sign is positive because profit exceeds cash (C).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A firm reports a net loss of $\\text{RM}5$ million and stockholders' equity of $-\\text{RM}20$ million. A screen computes its ROE as $25\\%$ and ranks it among the most profitable firms. What is the problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> None: a $25\\%$ ROE is strong, and the screen is right to rank the firm among the most profitable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The ROE should simply be reported as $-25\\%$, since the firm made a loss during the year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> With negative equity the ratio is meaningless; exclude the firm or use other measures.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Negative equity makes the firm safer, which the high ROE correctly reflects in the ranking.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notes warn that negative or very small equity makes debt-equity misleading; the same applies to ROE and P/B. Flipping the sign (B) is arbitrary, because the ratio has no economic meaning when the base is negative. Negative equity means liabilities exceed assets: more risk, not less (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A pitch says: \"The target trades at a P/E of $30$ with $25\\%$ consensus EPS growth, so its PEG is $1.2$: fairly priced.\" The consensus forecast covers only the next two years, after which growth is expected to settle near $8\\%$. Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree: a PEG close to $1$ means the share price is in line with expected growth, as the slide's rule of thumb says.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Disagree: the PEG is really $\\dfrac{30}{0.25} = 120$, which shows that the stock is grossly overvalued.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The P/E is irrelevant once growth is known.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Disagree: two years of $25\\%$ then $8\\%$ is not a 3- to 5-year rate; on $8\\%$ the PEG is $3.75$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>PEG is only as good as its growth input and horizon. B uses the decimal convention, which the notes warn against mixing with the percentage-point one. The slide's thresholds around $1$ are rules of thumb, not proof of fair value (A).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A firm with equity of $100$ and net income of $15$ (ROE $15\\%$) borrows $40$ at $5\\%$ and uses it to buy back shares. With tax at $25\\%$, net income falls by $40 \\times 5\\% \\times 0.75 = 1.5$ to $13.5$, and equity falls to $60$. ROE becomes $22.5\\%$. Evaluate the claim that \"the buyback created value because ROE rose by $7.5$ points\".</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: a rise in ROE always means that more value has been created for shareholders, whatever caused it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mostly mechanical leverage: ROE rose although net income fell, so value depends on debt cost and risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Impossible: ROE must have fallen, because net income fell from $15$ to $13.5$ after the extra interest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buybacks do not change equity, so ROE cannot have changed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\dfrac{13.5}{60} = 22.5\\%$: the smaller denominator outweighs the lower numerator. The equity multiplier rose, and so did the risk borne by each ringgit of equity. This is the notes' warning that \"higher ROE alone is not proof of better operations\", applied to a financing decision.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An \"industry average P/E\" is built from three firms with P/Es of $10$, $12$ and $200$; the last has EPS close to zero. Which benchmark is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The arithmetic mean, $74$, because every firm in the industry should count equally in a benchmark.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The highest value, $200$, as a conservative ceiling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The mean of the two lowest values, $11$, because a high P/E is always a data error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The harmonic mean ($\\approx 15.9$) or the median ($12$): the mean is dominated by one outlier.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\dfrac{3}{1/10 + 1/12 + 1/200} \\approx 15.9$; the harmonic mean averages earnings yields, which stay well behaved near zero EPS. The notes warn that near-zero EPS makes P/E \"unhelpful or unstable\". Dropping firms because their P/E is high (C) throws away information and is not a rule.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A study tests whether slide 7-54's warning signs predict share returns. It forms portfolios on each firm's fiscal year-end date, using that year's financial statements, and measures returns over the next twelve months. Which flaw most threatens the result?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Look-ahead bias: statements are published after the year-end, so returns must start from the filing date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Using too many firms in the sample makes the statistical test less reliable and the results noisier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The slide's ratio definitions differ from those used by some commercial data providers, biasing the ratios.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Firms from different industries are pooled in the same sample, so industry effects drive the result.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A tradable test can only use public information. Definition differences (C) and industry mixing (D) matter and should be handled, for example with industry-adjusted ratios, but look-ahead bias can manufacture apparent predictability on its own. More firms (B) improves power.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>You are designing a composite \"trouble-brewing\" score based on slide 7-54. Which design best captures the warning signs while avoiding problems of scale and definition?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Add up the raw ringgit increases in receivables and inventory for each firm, so that the largest absolute build-ups receive the highest trouble scores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use the P/E ratio alone, because the market price already contains every warning sign.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Within each industry and year, standardise receivables growth minus sales growth, inventory growth minus sales growth, the change in the current ratio, the change in debt-equity on one consistent definition, and $\\dfrac{NI - CFO}{\\text{average assets}}$; then combine the standardised scores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Rank firms on ROE, since the least profitable firms by that measure are the ones most likely to be heading for financial trouble.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>C turns each slide 7-54 sign into a scale-free measure, compares firms only with industry peers (the chapter's peer-comparison advice) and fixes the definitions. A rewards size, B assumes the market has already priced everything, and D would rank the highly leveraged firms (high ROE) as the safest.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
